F204 — an out-of-range shift diverges only when the count folds

[expr.shift]/1: shifting by a count that is negative or >= the width of the promoted left operand is
undefined behaviour. Neither backend is wrong, but clang answers the same expression two ways
depending on whether the count folds — 0 folded, `1 << (n mod 64)` at runtime — so there is no
semantics to match, only a build that must not silently pick one.

Refuses it where the count is a constant, which is the case a compiler can see. A runtime count stays
UB and keeps wasm's masking, exactly as clang's runtime path does. The bound is the promoted operand
width, so `(uint8)1 << 20` is still well-defined and still compiles.

This rejects code that builds today. That is the point: it is fail-closed, not a chosen answer.

Measured: ConstShift now `error: shift count 254 is out of range for a 64-bit operand`; in-range
controls unchanged. Suite 1085/0.

